  "preamble": "Debuccalization of the voiced aspirated dental stop /dʱ/ to the voiced glottal fricative [ɦ] between vowels is one of the regular Middle Indo-Aryan lenitions. In the Prakrits single intervocalic voiced aspirates (gʱ, dʱ, bʱ) lost their oral closure and only the breathy component survived as h [ɦ], so Sanskrit dadhi 'curd' became Prakrit dahi and vadhū 'bride' became vahū. Initial and geminate dʱ were kept. The h forms continue into the modern languages (Hindi bahū, bahrā).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Lenition, Intervocalic Weakening",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Prakrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit दधि dádhi 'curd'",
          "to": "Prakrit 𑀤𑀳𑀺 dahi 'curd'",
          "note": "Wiktionary also lists the alternative form 𑀤𑀥𑀺 dadhi; Dhivehi ދަހީ dahī continues the h form."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit वधू vadhū́ 'bride, wife'",
          "to": "Maharashtri Prakrit 𑀯𑀳𑀽 vahū",
          "note": "Sauraseni has both 𑀯𑀳𑀽 vahū and 𑀯𑀥𑀽 vadhū; Hindi बहू bahū 'daughter-in-law' continues vahū."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit बधिर badhira 'deaf'",
          "to": "Maharashtri, Sauraseni and Magadhi Prakrit 𑀩𑀳𑀺𑀭 bahira",
          "note": "Continued in Hindi बहरा bahrā, Marathi बहिरा bahirā and Bengali বহিরা bahirā."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
